e been  negotiations in the last few days  obviously they have fallen by the  wayside now they have failed  afghanistan's future was one of the  topics discussed during a meeting  between the british and pakistani  foreign ministers dominic rabb says the  uk is boosting aid for afghanistan's  neighbours as they deal with refugees  fleeing the country and his pakistani  counterpart shah mahmud gureshi says  that he'll stand with any afghan  government that's backed by the people  there are certain compulsions that we  have to deal with that perhaps you don't  have to for example  there are daily crossings of about 20 to  25 000 people in normal times  that's a huge number  can we block them  no we can't  can we regulate them yes we should uh  are there risks yes they are there are  organizations over there that are not  friendly to you us and or anyone so we  have regard against that as well  bulk of their trade  goes through pakistan  do we keep our borders open  can we close them and if we do close  them  are we contributing to a humanitarian  crisis over there  right  and  if we have to trade with them  then who do we talk to  anyone who's in charge  engaging with that authority is a  compulsion uh that we have to deal with  so  recognizing  these  challenges  pakistan said  it's for afghans  to decide about their future  we will engage with a a a  a government  that has the backing of the people of  afghanistan  well osama bin laden is in bashar and  pakistan has more on the foreign  minister's meeting  there is a lot of emphasis on pakistan's  facilitation  that's something that was discussed  between dominic raab and uh foreign  minister shaman with qureshi they also  discussed about how the uk can help  pakistan to cope with this crisis as you  heard the the foreign minister making  the case for pakistan we've we've been  speaking to him in the last few weeks  and he told me as well that pakistan has  been hosting a number of refugees  millions of them rather 